# Launchpad

The goal of Launchpad (LP) is for each graduate to discover a Next Great Adventure — a superpower skill, industry, and place to live and work — where he or she will find a calling that will change the world.

By Launchpad, the right heroes have developed the skills, drive, and character needed to change the world. If learners and owners hold the line on standards in an Acton Middle School studio, and only the most dedicated heroes from outside of Acton are admitted, Launchpad can be a dream come true.

While parents and adult experts, coaches, and mentors have a role to play in the lives of all Acton learners, Launchpadders usually have little need for a Guide and often are far more effective serving as part-time Guides in lower studios than adults.

Most Lanchpadders will complete most of the work in a traditional high school, or for college preparation, in two years or so. This allows time for: 

- Completing deep work in Apprenticeships

- Delivering Next Great Adventure Badges to excel in the real world

- Serving in leadership roles in Launchpad, Middle School, Elementary, and Spark studios; and the local community and Acton network



### Returning to the "Why?"

One of the challenges in Launchpad is keeping a cohesive culture when so many learners are pursuing individual Next Great Adventures and callings. Therefore, it is important for Launchpad Sheepdogs to keep returning to the “Why?” of Launchpad, which for many campuses is to support each other in finding a Next Great Adventure.



### Life after Launchpad

Colleges and universities need Launchpadders far more than Launchpadders need college, as most are prepared for fulfilling and rewarding jobs and lives at graduation. 

All across the network we see the 50% or so of Launchpadders who decide to attend college, admitted to a college or graduate school that fits — including some of the most prestigious universities in the world. 

However, the wise owner learns that trumpeting college success not only takes credit away from the Launchpadders who have done the work but can attract the wrong sort of parent who seeks prestige over competence.


